# CODEOWNERS — Four-in-a-Box ownership overlay for UIF.
#
# Domains are vertical, ownable units; capabilities are horizontal standards.
# The FDE owns a domain's engineering roots (src/ + infrastructure/); the UX
# Lead owns ui/ + ux/; the QE Lead owns qe/ and the CI green-gate; the PM owns
# scope (README.md/CLAUDE.md) and the Jira trace. Replace the placeholder teams
# with real handles.
#
# Later, more specific rules win.

# --- Central platform (standards, not code) --------------------------------
/architecture-design/     @uif-platform
/standards/               @uif-platform
/capabilities/            @uif-platform
/delivery-lab/            @uif-platform
/delivery-lab/reference-implementations/ @uif-platform
/_domain-template/        @uif-platform
/create-domain.sh         @uif-platform
/.github/                 @uif-platform
/jira/                    @uif-pm

# --- Domains: FDE owns the whole domain by default -------------------------
/domains/                 @uif-fde

# --- Cross-domain role overrides (UX + QE own their facets everywhere) -----
/domains/*/ui/            @uif-ux
/domains/*/ux/            @uif-ux
/domains/*/qe/            @uif-qe

# --- Stacks: same Four-in-a-Box overlay as domains, deliberately mirrored --
# A stack (echo/beacon/harbor-shaped — see stacks/harbor/CLAUDE.md) is not a
# domain, but ownership follows the identical pattern: FDE owns the whole
# stack by default, UX/QE own their facets. Without this, a stack silently
# has NO CODEOWNERS coverage at all (confirmed gap — stacks/harbor/ had none
# until this rule existed), since none of the /domains/ patterns above match
# a /stacks/ path.
/stacks/                  @uif-fde
/stacks/*/ui/             @uif-ux
/stacks/*/ux/             @uif-ux
/stacks/*/qe/             @uif-qe

# --- Echo: named accountable owner (ADR-0015, decided 2026-09-09) ----------
# Every @uif-* handle above is a PLACEHOLDER and none of them resolve — all
# five return 404 against this org. GitHub silently ignores an unresolvable
# owner, so the overlay above currently grants nothing and no path in this
# repository has working CODEOWNERS coverage. The entry below is the first one
# that does.
#
# Placed LAST on purpose. GitHub applies the last matching pattern, not the
# most specific one (the header's "more specific rules win" holds only because
# the two orders happen to coincide above). So this line deliberately takes
# stacks/echo/ui/, /ux/ and /qe/ back from the inert role globs rather than
# leaving those three facets owned by nobody. Split them out again once the UX
# and QE handles exist — the overlay is right, its handles just aren't real yet.
/stacks/echo/             @vaibhav-yenorkar_pearson
